{
  "dom_id": "step-e6806",
  "name": "Example: Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/7d62ee465505708dc91f486cf57953dc5058ad275fb5462b7217114d5c3e8ea0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down.",
  "worked_solution": "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($\\color{black}{\\frown}$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/ad6e25c0611cda21ffcd41114afc4d601779edb2f31288ab16dd31207bf83f65.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
